<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n1840" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">ălĭēnĭtas</span></b> ,
			ātis, f.
		alienus. In medic. lang., <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b>
			<i>The causes</i> or <i>materials of
				disease in the human system</i>, <i>which are
				extraneous to it</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">auferre alienitatem,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cael.
					Aur. Tard. 5, 4</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">alienitatis obtrusio,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					ib. 5, 64</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">manens alienitas,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id. Acut. 3, 20</span></i>
			.—</div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b> For
			alienatio (q. v. B.): 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">mentis,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Cael.
					Aur. Acut. 2, 39</span></i>
			.</div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
